add('Threshold completion counts certify a population and its optimum','ROBDD recurrence','2026-10-12','T','Exact constrained counting',
'What recurrence counts every feasible selection once, including skipped variables, and what makes an optimum certificate complete?',
'The Petersen records give capacities and historical totals, but not the fifty coefficient and profit rows needed to replay them. The rigorous core is a threshold dynamic program with explicit terminal multiplicities. A small instance below independently verifies counting and uniqueness while preserving each original project coordinate.',
['For n distinct projects, let w_i∈Z_≥0^d and capacity b∈Z^d. A binary selection x is feasible if Σw_i x_i≤b coordinatewise.','Let C(i,b) count feasible suffix assignments from coordinate i. A true terminal before the last variable represents every assignment of all remaining coordinates.'],
'Counting recurrence and optimization certificate',
'C(i,b)=0 if any coordinate of b is negative, C(n,b)=1 for b≥0, and C(i,b)=C(i+1,b)+C(i+1,b−w_i). If b dominates the entire suffix weight, C(i,b)=2^(n−i). For integer profits, uniqueness at value v follows from one feasible witness of value v and exactly one feasible assignment with value at least v.',
'Partition the feasible suffixes by x_i=0 or x_i=1. These disjoint sets are respectively the two smaller capacity problems, proving the recurrence inductively from its terminal cases. If every suffix assignment fits, there are n−i freely chosen bits, so its contribution is a power of two, not one. A reduced decision diagram may omit coordinates on which the residual predicate does not depend; each such free bit still doubles the represented assignments. Equivalently, an edge skipping g variables carries multiplicity 2ᵍ. For the optimization statement, the threshold count excludes every assignment of higher profit and every second assignment of equal profit. Conversely a unique maximizer makes that threshold population a singleton. The coefficient-generating product ∏_i(1+z^(w_i)t^(p_i)) gives the same count by selecting either monomial from each distinct project factor; equal weight vectors do not identify the underlying selections.',
'Use weights (2,3,4), capacity five and profits (3,4,5).',
'The feasible bit strings are 000,001,010,100,110, so the population is five. Their profits are 0,5,4,3,7; the unique optimum is 110 at seven, and the profit-at-least-seven population is one. A separate predicate x₀∧x₂ on four coordinates has four solutions because x₁ and x₃ are free, illustrating skipped-variable multiplicity.',
'The recurrence and a complete small optimum certificate are replayed independently.',
'The historical 702,508,860,122,761 total and 16,537 optimum remain source claims here; a theorem about the recurrence is not a replay of an absent concrete DAG or coefficient table. Variable ordering can change computational cost while preserving the mathematical population.',
'Emit a certificate DAG whose nodes carry variable indices, residual capacities and exact counts; check every recurrence edge, terminal power and root count, then add a profit-threshold singleton certificate linked to the original project identities.',
[('Bryant: graph-based Boolean-function algorithms','https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6605990')])
add('Completion counts make ranking, inverse ranking and sampling exact','exact counts enable rank','2026-10-12','A','Finite population algorithms',
'How do local completion counts yield a global bijection between feasible objects and integer addresses?',
'The recovered query-space work emphasizes exact addresses, inverse lookup, pagination and uniform sampling. These are consequences of a single prefix-partition theorem. It is the disjoint completion population, not the apparent size of an ambient search box, that determines valid addresses.',
['Let Ω⊆{0,1}ⁿ be a finite nonempty population ordered lexicographically with 0 before 1. For a prefix q, let C(q) count its feasible completions.','The empty prefix has C(∅)=|Ω| and C(q)=C(q0)+C(q1); a feasible complete word has count one.'],
'Rank/unrank bijection and unbiased sampling',
'There is an exact bijection rank:Ω→{0,…,|Ω|−1}. At prefix q, all q0 completions occupy the first C(q0) addresses; the q1 block occupies the following C(q1). A uniform address therefore gives a uniform object.',
'At a node q, split its contiguous address interval into disjoint initial and final blocks with those lengths. For unranking, compare the residual address r with C(q0): choose 0 when r<C(q0), otherwise choose 1 and replace r by r−C(q0). The chosen block has positive length and its residual address is valid; induction reaches exactly one feasible leaf. For ranking, scan a feasible word and add C(q0) whenever its next bit is 1. At each step these operations are inverse translations on the two disjoint blocks, proving the global bijection. Consequently P(unrank(R)=ω)=1/|Ω| when R is uniform. Conditional populations use the same construction below a fixed prefix. To draw an exact uniform integer from random bits, rejection sampling of a b-bit integer with 2ᵇ≥|Ω| is unbiased; reducing that integer modulo |Ω| is generally biased unless the modulus divides 2ᵇ.',
'Rank the five feasible words from the three-project example.',
'The list is 000,001,010,100,110 with ranks 0,…,4; the prefix 1 has two completions. To sample one of five objects, generate three fair bits and reject addresses 5,6,7. Each valid address has conditional probability 1/5. Direct reduction of all eight bit strings modulo five instead gives probabilities (2,2,2,1,1)/8 and is not uniform.',
'The population has a reversible exact index and a precisely stated sampling distribution.',
'A deterministic seed supplies reproducibility, not by itself a proof of uniformity or cryptographic unpredictability. If distinct source objects were merged, the address bijection would index the quotient rather than the original population.',
'Use counted prefixes as a persistent paginated API: each page is a half-open rank interval, and every update carries a versioned population definition so old addresses cannot silently refer to different objects.',
[('Bryant: graph-based Boolean-function algorithms','https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6605990')])
add('Factor elimination preserves counts only when auxiliary fibres are controlled','functional gates requiring','2026-10-12','A','Factor graphs and projection',
'Why does eliminating a variable preserve a partition sum, and when does introducing a functional gate preserve the original model count?',
'The source distinguishes sparse elimination from implementation benchmarks and records a gate-projection bijection. Both can be proved directly by finite sums. Auxiliary variables are harmless only when their number of extensions is exactly controlled for every original assignment.',
['Let variables range over finite sets and let Z=Σ_x∏_j f_j(x_scope(j)) over a commutative semiring, such as nonnegative integers.','For a Boolean relation F(x,a), let m(x)=|{a:F(x,a)}| be the auxiliary fibre size.'],
'Exact elimination and unique-extension projection',
'Eliminating v replaces all factors involving v by g(y)=Σ_v∏_(j:v∈scope(j))f_j, leaving other factors unchanged and preserving Z. If F(x,a) encodes a predicate P(x) with exactly one auxiliary extension for each satisfying x and none otherwise, then #F=#P.',
'In the finite sum defining Z, factors independent of v can be moved outside the sum over v by distributivity. The remaining sum is precisely g, proving elimination. Repeating this operation in any order preserves the same partition sum, although intermediate scopes and cost can differ greatly. For projection, partition the satisfying assignments of F by their original x-coordinate. Its count is Σ_x m(x), which equals #P exactly when m(x)=1 on P and zero off P. Merely proving existence of an auxiliary extension proves equisatisfiability, not count equality. If the fibre size is a known constant c on P, the count is c#P and a correction is possible; varying multiplicity needs weights or explicit projection. The argument also explains why duplicate-looking original coordinates must not be merged without their multiplicities.',
'Introduce the gate a=x₁ XOR x₂.',
'All four binary choices of (x₁,x₂) have exactly one admissible a, so the gate relation has four satisfying triples. If a is instead unconstrained, the count is eight although both formulas project to the same four original assignments. Adding the condition a=1 gives precisely the two original inputs 01 and 10. Eliminating a by summing its gate indicator times its output indicator yields the XOR indicator on the original variables.',
'The count-preserving gate theorem is verified on every fibre rather than inferred from satisfiability.',
'The source’s benchmark solve counts and timing comparisons require raw instances and execution receipts; the algebraic theorem does not reproduce them. Real-valued signed factors can define a partition sum without defining a probability distribution.',
'Generate elimination certificates as exact local tables or symbolic factors, attach a unique-extension proof to each auxiliary gate, and check the final scalar independently of the optimizer used to choose elimination order.',
[('Bryant: graph-based Boolean-function algorithms','https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/6605990')])
add('A Hankel witness proves the minimum observable recurrence order','six-state-to-three-state','2026-10-10','A','Linear realization theory',
'What proves that a reduced state model preserves every output and that no smaller exact recurrence exists?',
'The source reports a six-to-three-state reduction with a nonsingular Hankel witness. Since its matrices are absent, the following explicit six-state model is newly chosen. It illustrates both halves of a complete certificate: an all-time intertwiner for correctness and a Hankel minor for minimality.',
['For a sequence y_n=cAⁿb over Q, its Hankel matrix is H_ij=y_(i+j), i,j≥0.','A forward recurrence of order r means y_(n+r)=Σ_(j=0)^(r−1)a_j y_(n+j) for every n≥0, with constant coefficients.'],
'Intertwining plus rank gives exact minimality',
'If RA=GR, Rb=β and c=dR, then y_n=dGⁿβ for every n≥0. Any r-state realization or order-r recurrence has Hankel rank at most r. Thus a nonzero r×r Hankel minor together with an r-state realization proves minimal dimension and recurrence order exactly r.',
'From RA=GR, induction gives RAⁿ=GⁿR; the input and output identities then yield the full sequence equality, not merely agreement on a sampled prefix. For an s-state realization, H_ij=(cAⁱ)(Aʲb), so every finite Hankel block factors through an s-dimensional space and has rank at most s. An order-s recurrence similarly expresses every later Hankel column as a fixed combination of the preceding s columns. Hence either model needs at least the size of a nonzero minor. Conversely, an r-dimensional realization supplies an order at most r through its characteristic polynomial by Cayley–Hamilton. This suffices for the certificate used here; general minimal realization can be constructed from the shift action on the finite-dimensional span of Hankel columns.',
'Take A=diag(1,2,3,4,5,6), b=(1,1,1,0,0,0)ᵀ and c=bᵀ.',
'Projection R onto the first three coordinates gives G=diag(1,2,3), β=(1,1,1)ᵀ and d=(1,1,1). Thus y_n=1+2ⁿ+3ⁿ, starting 3,6,14,36,98. Its leading 3×3 Hankel determinant is four, the squared Vandermonde determinant on 1,2,3. The recurrence is y_(n+3)=6y_(n+2)−11y_(n+1)+6y_n, from (t−1)(t−2)(t−3). Therefore three is exact.',
'A global matrix identity and a finite nonzero determinant jointly certify the reduction and its optimality.',
'No finite prefix alone rules out a later perturbation unless a model class or global recurrence is already proved. Approximate low rank and a small singular value do not establish exact state equivalence.',
'For a supplied rational automaton, compute reachable and observable quotients over Q, emit the intertwining matrices and a nonsingular Hankel minor, and connect the certificate to every original input and output channel.',
[('Balle et al.: canonical forms for weighted automata','https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.06841')])
